Data
The formula functions take named columns: an associative array of column =&gt; list of values, with every column the same length. A table object with toModelData() or toColumns(), such as a phibble DataFrame, is also accepted.
use function Phit\{fit_ols, predict};

$flats = [
    'rent' =&gt; [
        1879.25, 1805.03, 2022.29, 1267.85, 1509.24,
        2240.10, 959.51, 2386.54, 1655.80, 1420.00,
    ],
    'area' =&gt; [
        74.4, 57.4, 66.4, 49.1, 45.7,
        92.4, 37.0, 93.9, 61.2, 52.3,
    ],
    'district' =&gt; [
        'north', 'south', 'north', 'east', 'south',
        'north', 'east', 'south', 'east', 'south',
    ],
];

Numeric values make a numeric column.
Strings make a factor whose levels sort in byte order, so east is the reference level.
Model formulas lists every accepted column type.

Fit
$fit = fit_ols('rent ~ area + district', $flats);
rent is the response. area + district adds a slope for floor area and treatment-coded indicators for the districts. The intercept is included unless the formula removes it with - 1 or + 0.

Predict
predict() rebuilds the formula over new data. The response column is not needed, and factor levels are those the fit saw.
$new = [
    'area' =&gt; [55.0, 80.0],
    'district' =&gt; ['east', 'north'],
];
$p = predict($fit, $new, interval: 'prediction');

foreach ($p['fit'] as $i =&gt; $value) {
    printf(
        "%.2f [%.2f, %.2f]\n",
        $value,
        $p['lower'][$i],
        $p['upper'][$i],
    );
}
1407.55 [991.59, 1823.51]
2090.69 [1676.97, 2504.41]

interval: 'prediction' covers one new flat’s rent. interval: 'confidence' covers the mean rent.
A district the fit never saw throws an exception with code 1001.
Prediction covers generalized linear and mixed-model predictions.

Declare the dependency
Declare the extension in composer.json so that Composer reports a missing extension at install time:
{
    "require": {
        "ext-phit": "*"
    }
}
The polyfill also satisfies ext-phit. See Extension or polyfill.

A random-intercept model
The example uses docs/data/schools.csv: 250 simulated students in 12 schools, with study hours, a prior score, and a test score. read_csv() needs the extension; with the polyfill, read the file another way.
use function Phit\{fit_mixed_gaussian, read_csv};

$csv = read_csv('docs/data/schools.csv');
$schools = array_combine(
    $csv['names'],
    array_column($csv['columns'], 'values'),
);

$fit = fit_mixed_gaussian(
    'score ~ study_hours + prior_score + (1 | school)',
    $schools,
);
foreach ($fit['columns'] as $i =&gt; $name) {
    printf(
        "%-12s %8.4f %8.4f\n",
        $name,
        $fit['coefficients'][$i],
        $fit['std_errors'][$i],
    );
}
$school = $fit['random'][0];
printf(
    "%s: school SD %.4f, residual SD %.4f\n",
    $fit['method'],
    sqrt($school['covariance'][0][0]),
    $fit['sigma'],
);
printf(
    "%s %+.4f\n",
    $school['group_labels'][0],
    $school['effects'][0][0],
);
(Intercept)   46.6627   2.2438
study_hours    1.6141   0.1609
prior_score    0.2453   0.0419
REML: school SD 4.4579, residual SD 5.0566
school-01 +0.1353
A school’s effect is its predicted intercept relative to the average school, shrunk toward zero.
